{
  "version": 269,
  "released": "2026-08-30",
  "changelog": "v269: EVENT IDENTITY I2 — WHAT THE FIRST REAL VAULT SAID. v268 shipped the binder with eighty-one tests and a nine-telling synthetic fixture. Then it was run, read-only, against a clone of the founder's own vault — 883 tellings, rollout step 1's own dry run, nothing applied — and it found four defects the fixture could not have shown, because every telling in the fixture was a classifier telling with a place, a date and a sentence for a label. This release is those four fixes and the honest verdict on the fifth question.\n\nONE PAIR, ONE ROW. R1 has to judge BOTH directions: four of its seven conditions are asymmetric (a repeatable-kind episode protects itself only in the direction that reaches it) and a bind requires both sides to have chosen each other. But it was REPORTING both, so 4 164 \"pairs\" were 2 082 pairs counted twice, and a `same_event` emission would have minted two work items for one question. The directions are still judged and still kept; the report, the questions and every count now collapse to one row per unordered pair. The surviving row is chosen by a stated rule — a telling measured against an EXISTING episode is the canonical direction, otherwise the lower telling ref — it carries what either direction refused, and a refusal in either direction refuses the pair. The collapse takes a COPY, so the judged directions stay a faithful record of what each one decided. §6.1's \"one pair per telling at a time\" now counts a pair against BOTH of its units, because after the collapse a telling is the named side of only some of the pairs it is in.\n\nONE TALLY. The summary counted `proposals` off the containment RECORDS while the verdict line counted `proposal` off the pair rows, and on the founder's vault they printed `proposals: 0` directly above `proposal=16`. Two tallies of one word is a report nobody can trust. There is one tally now, over the collapsed pairs, and the fields are named for what they actually count: `proposal_pairs` and `part_of_records`, with `directions_judged` printed beside `pairs` so \"why is this twice that\" has an answer on the page.\n\nTHE AGE FRAMES ARE THE FOLD'S. The vault reported `age_frames: 0` while holding a day-precision birthday, and the `bounds_in_frame` retrieval signal was therefore dead on all 883 tellings. The cause is one this repo has a doctrine about: subjects resolve INSIDE the fold (v221), so a raw claim carries `subject_ref: None` and the owner's own birthday arrives as `subject_mention: \"birth\"` — and the binder had written its own owner-birth predicate over raw claims, looking for `self`/`me`/`i`, which matched nothing. `CalculatedTimeline` now hands back the frames it calculated and the binder reads them. The field is IN-MEMORY ONLY — in neither `to_dict` nor `structural_signature` — so nothing is published, no fingerprint moves and `PROJECTION_SCHEMA_VERSION` does not budge. The honest cost is one fold pass per binder run, on a maintenance step that is never a turn and never a compile. The FIXTURE was wrong in the same way and hid it: its owner birth said `\"I\"`, which the fold does not resolve to the owner, and only the binder's private predicate ever accepted it. One definition means the fixture has to be right too.\n\nTHE RECORDER'S STEM. A recorder telling's SUBJECT is the thing itself — `Etherfuse`, started, 2022-05 — so subtracting the cast from the label, which is right for a classifier sentence like \"Co-founded Etherfuse with AJ\", erased the whole label and left a stem of `\"\"`. A telling with no stem matches nothing, so EVERY DATED LANDMARK ROW ON THE VAULT WAS UNMATCHABLE BY CONSTRUCTION — the exact rows that can date the undated classifier occurrences, which is the entire diagnosis the program was commissioned from. Two fixes, both in one function: the cast is evidence BESIDE the label and never INSTEAD of it, so a subtraction that would empty the stem leaves it alone; and the act is read from `event_kind` when the words do not carry it, because the recorder puts the act in a field and the classifier puts it in a sentence — one rule, read from wherever a source kind keeps it. That makes a recorder stem MORE specific, never less. Twelve landmark pairs and nine stem matches became visible that had not existed at all.\n\nA LABEL IS NOT ALSO A PARTICIPANT. Falling out of the above: on a recorder telling the subject is at once the label and the only \"participant\", and condition 4 counts participants as INDEPENDENT evidence. Nine live pairs reported `1 of 2: participant` where the participant and the matched stem were the same token — one fact satisfying two signals the condition has the word independent in it to prevent. A shared participant whose tokens are already in the matched label is no longer evidence. Strictly more conservative: it moved those nine to `0 of 2: none` and changed no verdict.\n\nR1'S FLOOR IS UNCHANGED, AND ZERO BINDS IS THE HONEST ANSWER. Not one pair on the founder's vault binds, and that is the approved floor working rather than failing. All seventeen stem-matching pairs carry at most ONE independent, non-label, non-owner signal — eight classifier pairs agree on a place and nothing else, eight recorder pairs agree on nothing once the label stops counting twice, one keeps a genuine participant — so each is a proposal or a question and none is a bind. The gap that produces it is named rather than papered over: the dated landmark and the undated classifier occurrences of one company share only the entity's NAME, and a name match is one signal. The single change that would give those pairs a real second signal is a RESOLVED-ENTITY signal — v221's own subject resolution reused as retrieval evidence — and that is a design decision for the owner, not a threshold to quietly lower. It is written down in ADR 0031 and nowhere in the code.\n\nPROVEN TO FIRE. Eleven further one-line mutations were each applied to a built tree and the suite seen going red: the report keeps both directions; the collapse rewrites the judged direction it summarizes; a bind in either direction wins the pair; the canonical direction ignores the existing episode; the summary counts records under the verdict's name; the caps count only the named telling; the binder derives its own age frames; the fold stops handing its frames back; the cast is subtracted down to nothing; the act is never read from the kind; a label counts as its own participant. Two of the eleven were green on the first attempt and named real holes in the tests — a fixture where the two tally fields happened to coincide, and an assertion comparing pairs by value where it meant identity — and both guards were rewritten against a state where they actually fire.",
